COMMIT e835f43d36b69f3378f938e8124b60444eaa8d1e
fix(core): FIX-71 BatchingWriter catches Error, and a refused window commits

D-104 as decided: the catches at BatchingWriter:156 and :298 widened to
`RuntimeException | Error`, one take and one arm attempt per drain, and the
batches a drain took before a refused window are COMMITTED rather than failed --
they stay in flight, reach the sink in the order taken, and what stayed queued
rides their completions. Adds the `windowsRefused` counter and its episode
WARNINGs; removes `Refused`.

Two test changes D-104 did not name, called out here because the rule is that an
implementer may change a test it did not write only to fix a wrong expectation,
with the reason in the commit:
- BatchingWriterWindowRefusalTest's `assertEverySettled` went from "not sunk
  implies failed with the refusal" to "every item sunk and succeeded". That is
  strictly stronger, and the old helper would still pass against the new code; it
  is replaced because it encoded FIX-69's superseded policy as a helper.
- The FIX-71 packet's case 7, oneDrainHandsItsBatchesToTheSinkOldestFirst, could
  not be written. A drain provably takes at most one batch, so there is nothing to
  order. It is replaced by ErrorTest's
  theConcatenationOfTheBatchesInHandOffOrderIsArrivalOrderAcrossQueueAndWaiters,
  which states that reason.

Five review rounds. The fourth is the one worth recording, because it overturned
my own judgement rather than the author's code. I had declared the survivor at
:185 an equivalent mutant. It is not: remove `cancelQuietly(armed)` and the window
flush dropped stays scheduled, later sets a newer window's `armed` to null and
hands a newer partial batch to the sink early. The reviewer supplied the timing
scenario, it is now
aWindowThatFlushDroppedNeverFiresSoANewerPartialBatchWaitsForItsOwnWindow, and the
mutant dies on its t50 assertion. The reviewer also named the fault in my
reasoning -- "no item outcome differs" taken to mean "unobservable" -- which is the
same model that made my prediction for mutant 7 wrong in the other direction.

The second survivor, `.reversed()` at :272, IS equivalent, and the proof is an
invariant rather than an intuition: at rest either the queue is shorter than a
batch or inFlight is at the commit limit, and waiters exist only when the queue is
full, which implies the limit; so a drain takes at most one batch. That proof
exposed generality for a case that cannot happen -- a `while` that runs once, a
`.reversed()` that cannot reverse, a javadoc describing unreachable ordering --
which is FIX-137.

Also fixed in the last round: a vacuous step in the log test (its "further
accepted window" never actually got one accepted, so a log-recovery mutant
survived it) and a tautological half of an assertion that re-checked a value set
two lines above.

Evidence, all observed: `clean verify -Pit` exit 0, BUILD SUCCESS, 16 modules,
11:53 (agent/work/FIX-71-verify.log); coverage check exit 0, "coverage: no
decrease", core 99.6% line and 99.5% branch (FIX-71-coverage.log); scoped PIT api
179/180 and core 1019/1029, both 99% against the 80% floor (FIX-71-pit.log);
scoped suite 47/0/0/0 on the restored file (FIX-71-r5-final.log). 23 hand mutants,
all killed. BatchingWriter.java `cmp`-identical to the pre-mutant copy after every
mutant run.

Riding along in the ledger, stated rather than hidden: D-104 is amended twice from
this review (the cancelQuietly policy and why it carries no counter; and that a
dropped window may fire early, which costs batching and not data). D-111 is new
and belongs to FIX-133, not here -- recording is independent of handling. New rows
FIX-132 extended, FIX-135, FIX-136, FIX-137, FIX-138, and FIX-127 extended with
the log-under-lock site this review found. They are here because they came out of
this task's review and the next commit should not have to explain them.

