+| D-111 | 2026-11-14 | Recording is independent of handling (FIX-133, FIX-131 case 2), reversible. A failure is RECORDED even when it is deliberately not retried, not parked and not handled. `Recorder.wrap` catches `Exception` only (`Recorder.java:85`), so a step that throws an `Error` leaves no row at all and the timeline shows the step as one that never ran — measured by `RedeliveredTimelineTest` case 2, where a crashed first run plus a redelivery produces a timeline of steps 0-7 all `OK`, indistinguishable from one clean successful run. Monitoring is the product, so a failure the timeline cannot show is a product defect. The fix is narrow and changes NO control flow: widen that catch to `RuntimeException \| Error` as D-104 did in `BatchingWriter`, write the `FAILED` row with the throwable's text, and rethrow unchanged. Everything D-098 and D-049 decided about an `Error` stands — it is not transient, it is not retried, `FlowWorker` still leaves the item to its lease, and D-103's rule that a user error handler never sees an interrupt is untouched. Explicitly NOT changed: `Parking.java:49` catches `Exception` only and must keep doing so, because parking an `Error` would make it transient, which D-098 refused; the scout reading that line as a second instance of the same gap was wrong, and it is recorded here so nobody “fixes” it. Still open as its own row, because it contradicts D-049 (d) rather than this one: `Delivery.java:319-320` catches `RuntimeException` only around the ingress `intake.save`, so an `Error` escapes `apply()` where D-049 (d) promises “never an escaped exception”. | FIX-131, FIX-133 |
